.TH LIBMPQ__UPDATE_COMPACT 3 "2026-11-11" "libmpq 0.9.0" "libmpq Library Functions"
.SH NAME
libmpq__update_compact \- stage physical archive compaction
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <libmpq/mpq.h>

int32_t libmpq__update_compact(mpq_update_s *update);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Immediately reconstruct and validate the current transaction stage with densely
placed live payloads. This call neither publishes nor consumes the update
handle. Commit remains the sole publication operation. Invoke compaction after
other mutations when final placement matters; later changes can introduce gaps.
.PP
Supported members retain their compressed representation, flags, attributes,
FILETIME and identity associations. Shared classic aliases reference one
physical payload. Classic hash capacity, probe slots, locales, platforms and
deleted-slot semantics are preserved. Required metadata reservations and raw
arrays remain; neither minimum size nor a reduction in total bytes is promised.
.PP
A complete layout and eligibility check can prove that the archive is already
compact. Such a no-op preserves staged bytes and signatures without creating a
candidate. It does not imply successful plaintext verification of every member.
Actual reconstruction removes stale signatures and regenerates applicable
structural and raw MD5 using the original v4 raw-chunk policy.
.PP
Supported v1-v4 classic and representable HET/BET layouts reuse
identity-preserving packed reconstruction. Unknown unencrypted and
position-independent encrypted payloads can move as opaque stored bytes.
Ciphertext copying is not plaintext verification. KEY relocation requires
a usable basename key when its effective key changes; no name is fabricated.
Every shared encrypted alias requires a known, key-compatible spelling.
Ambiguous or insufficient shared encryption provenance is rejected.
.PP
Discoverable embedded prefixes are copied byte-for-byte, retaining the original
absolute MPQ-header position. Automatic discovery scans 512-byte boundaries or
follows one validated MPQ user-data header, which may point to an unaligned MPQ.
Opaque prefixes before a discoverable user-data header, its payload, extended
header bytes and padding are retained unchanged. Arbitrary payload bytes are
not scanned for incidental MPQ signatures. Unaligned origins without a
discoverable user-data pointer and contradictory prefix metadata are rejected.
Only supported MPQ relationships and prefix bytes are preserved; arbitrary
executable/container validity is not guaranteed. KEY adjustments use
MPQ-relative member offsets and never include prefix length.
.PP
Unrelated trailers, physical patch/delete members,
contradictory or reduced hashes, unsupported BET/attributes metadata and
overlapping physical ranges are rejected conservatively. Recognized strong
signature trailers survive proven no-ops and are removed on reconstruction.
Authenticated MPQE uses the existing plaintext stage and encrypted publication.
.PP
Temporary disk space is required for the working stage and a complete candidate,
in addition to the original archive. Embedded reconstruction also needs a
completed zero-origin MPQ temporary while assembling the full candidate.
MPQE publication can require another
encrypted candidate. Memory for payload copying is bounded independently of
unpacked file size. Cleanup remains best effort on persistent I/O failure;
secure deletion and crash durability are not guaranteed.
.PP
Semantic rejection preserves the preceding valid stage. Existing operational
adoption failures can leave the transaction abort-only. Prefix assembly is
validated before adoption; pre-adoption failures preserve the previous stage. Abort and failed
publication preserve the original under the normal transaction guarantees.
.PP
Named update reconstruction preserves the same supported embedded prefixes
without imposing dense placement. Ordinary legacy v1/v2 updates retain their
existing unrelated-suffix behavior. For MPQE, prefix preservation
refers to the decoded logical source, using existing MPQE re-encryption and publication.
.PP
Use \fBlibmpq__update_compact_ex\fP() to supply a synchronous array of known
filenames for unlisted encrypted KEY members and aliases. Its zero-count form
is identical to this function; caller names do not expand supported layouts.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Returns 0 on success, including proven no-ops, or a negative libmpq error.
.SH ERRORS
An invalid or abort-only handle returns LIBMPQ_ERROR_EXIST. Malformed or
unsupported layouts return LIBMPQ_ERROR_FORMAT. Unavailable required relocation
keys return LIBMPQ_ERROR_DECRYPT. Size, allocation, read, write, seek and close
failures propagate the established corresponding errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
